course: dictionary
lesson: 11624
title: Apricot: fruit or colour?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An apricot is a small, round, soft fruit with orange-yellow skin and a large stone inside. You can eat it fresh, or dry it to make dried apricots that you can snack on.
explain: The word can also name a colour. «Her new bedroom curtains were apricot.» Here apricot is not a fruit. It describes the curtains, and it means a pale orange-yellow, just like the fruit's skin.
explain: How do you know which one it means? Read the words around it. «He spread apricot jam on his toast.» Jam is made from fruit, so apricot is the fruit here. Always ask: does the sentence still make sense if I put in fruit or colour instead?
fact: An apricot is a small, round, soft fruit with orange-yellow skin.
fact: Inside an apricot there is a large stone.
fact: Apricot can be a noun for the fruit, or an adjective for the colour.
fact: As a colour, apricot is a pale orange-yellow.
fact: Useful phrases: «dried apricots», «apricot jam», «apricot tree».
exercise: «She ate three fresh apricots after school.» What is an apricot in this sentence? | A small animal | *A fruit | A colour | A type of bread
exercise: «The walls of the bedroom were apricot.» What does apricot describe here? | A kind of jam | *A pale orange-yellow colour | A soft, round fruit | A tall, old tree
exercise: «Dad spread apricot jam on the warm toast.» What does apricot tell you in this sentence? | It means the jam is cold | It is a type of bread | *It tells you what kind of jam it is | It is a person's name
test: «Mia painted her bedroom apricot.» Which word is the clue that apricot is a colour here? | *Painted | Her | Bedroom | Mia
test: «The apricot tree in our garden gave us lots of fruit this year.» What is apricot in this sentence? | A large stone | A kind of cake | *A tree that grows the fruit | A pale orange-yellow colour
exam: Which sentence uses apricot as a colour? | «The apricot tree grew tall in the garden.» | *«Her new hat was a soft apricot.» | «We bought some apricot jam at the shop.» | «He ate an apricot at break.»
